@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "AFTER DELETE Trigger";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            AFTER DELETE Trigger
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how AFTER DELETE Triggers work in SQL Server,
            how to create them and how to use the
            <strong>deleted</strong> logical table.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is an AFTER DELETE Trigger?
        </h2>

        <p>
            An <strong>AFTER DELETE Trigger</strong> is a special
            SQL Server database object that automatically executes
            after a successful <strong>DELETE</strong> operation
            on a table.
        </p>

        <p>
            It is useful when we want to perform an automatic action
            whenever records are deleted from a table.
        </p>

        <ul>

            <li>
                It executes after a DELETE operation.
            </li>

            <li>
                It can be used for auditing deleted records.
            </li>

            <li>
                It can maintain a history of deleted data.
            </li>

            <li>
                It can store information in an audit table.
            </li>

            <li>
                It can perform automatic database operations.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Basic Syntax
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER TriggerName
ON TableName
AFTER DELETE
AS
BEGIN

    -- Trigger logic

END;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         EXAMPLE TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Let's assume that we have a
            <strong>Students</strong> table.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE Students
(
    StudentId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         INSERT SAMPLE DATA
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Insert Sample Data
        </h2>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    101,
    'Rahul',
    'Pune',
    21
);</code></pre>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>INSERT INTO Students
(
    StudentId,
    StudentName,
    City,
    Age
)
VALUES
(
    102,
    'Priya',
    'Mumbai',
    22
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE AUDIT TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Create an Audit Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            Before creating the trigger, we can create a separate
            table to store the details of deleted students.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TABLE DeletedStudentAudit
(
    AuditId INT IDENTITY(1,1) PRIMARY KEY,
    StudentId INT,
    StudentName VARCHAR(100),
    City VARCHAR(50),
    Age INT,
    DeletedDate DATETIME
);</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE AFTER DELETE TRIGGER
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Creating an AFTER DELETE Trigger
        </h2>

        <p>
            Now we create a trigger that automatically stores
            deleted student information in the audit table.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_StudentAfterDelete
ON Students
AFTER DELETE
AS
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO DeletedStudentAudit
    (
        StudentId,
        StudentName,
        City,
        Age,
        DeletedDate
    )
    SELECT
        StudentId,
        StudentName,
        City,
        Age,
        GETDATE()
    FROM deleted;

END;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         DELETED LOGICAL TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            The deleted Logical Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            SQL Server provides a special logical table named
            <strong>deleted</strong> inside a DELETE trigger.
        </p>

        <p>
            The <strong>deleted</strong> logical table contains
            the rows that were deleted by the DELETE operation.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-light">

                    <tr>

                        <th>Logical Table</th>

                        <th>Contains</th>

                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>

                        <td>
                            <strong>deleted</strong>
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Rows that were deleted from the table
                        </td>

                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         DELETE OPERATION
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Performing a DELETE Operation
        </h2>

        <p>
            Now delete a student from the
            <strong>Students</strong> table.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         WHAT HAPPENS
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What Happens During DELETE?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose the student record before deletion is:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>StudentId = 101
StudentName = Rahul
City = Pune
Age = 21</code></pre>

        <p>
            When the following statement executes:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        <p>
            SQL Server makes the deleted row available through
            the <strong>deleted</strong> logical table.
        </p>

        <p>
            Our trigger then reads the deleted record and inserts
            it into the <strong>DeletedStudentAudit</strong> table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         CHECK AUDIT TABLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Check Deleted Records
        </h2>

        <p>
            To check the records saved by the trigger:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM DeletedStudentAudit;</code></pre>

        <p class="mt-3">
            The deleted student information will be available
            in the audit table.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE ROW DELETE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            AFTER DELETE Trigger with Multiple Rows
        </h2>

        <p>
            A DELETE statement can delete multiple records at once.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DELETE FROM Students
WHERE City = 'Mumbai';</code></pre>

        <p>
            In this situation, the <strong>deleted</strong> logical
            table can contain multiple rows.
        </p>

        <p>
            Therefore, trigger logic should be written using
            set-based operations and should not assume that only
            one record was deleted.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         DELETE AND INSERTED
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            deleted Table in DELETE Trigger
        </h2>

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <p class="mb-2">
                During a DELETE operation:
            </p>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li>
                    <strong>deleted</strong> contains the deleted rows.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The deleted rows can be accessed inside the trigger.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The deleted information can be copied to an
                    audit or history table.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         PRACTICAL EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            A common real-world use of an AFTER DELETE Trigger
            is maintaining an audit or history table.
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_StudentDeleteHistory
ON Students
AFTER DELETE
AS
BEGIN

    INSERT INTO DeletedStudentAudit
    (
        StudentId,
        StudentName,
        City,
        Age,
        DeletedDate
    )
    SELECT
        StudentId,
        StudentName,
        City,
        Age,
        GETDATE()
    FROM deleted;

END;</code></pre>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT NOTES
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Important
            </h5>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li>
                    AFTER DELETE triggers execute after a
                    successful DELETE operation.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The <strong>deleted</strong> logical table
                    contains the deleted rows.
                </li>

                <li>
                    A DELETE statement can affect multiple rows.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Trigger logic should therefore support
                    multiple deleted records.
                </li>

                <li>
                    AFTER DELETE triggers are commonly used for
                    auditing and maintaining deletion history.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h5 class="fw-bold">
                Summary
            </h5>

            <p class="mb-0">

                An <strong>AFTER DELETE Trigger</strong> automatically
                executes after a successful DELETE operation.
                The <strong>deleted</strong> logical table provides
                access to the records that were deleted. AFTER DELETE
                Triggers are commonly used for auditing, maintaining
                deletion history and performing automatic database
                operations.

            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


